{"abstract":"KPTN-related disorder is an autosomal recessive disorder associated with germline variants in KPTN (previously known as kaptin), a component of the mTOR regulatory complex KICSTOR. To gain further insights into the pathogenesis of KPTN-related disorder, we analysed mouse knockout and human stem cell KPTN loss-of-function models. Kptn-/- mice display many of the key KPTN-related disorder phenotypes, including brain overgrowth, behavioural abnormalities, and cognitive deficits. By assessment of affected individuals, we have identified widespread cognitive deficits (n=6) and postnatal onset of brain overgrowth (n=19). By analysing head size data from their parents (n=24), we have identified a previously unrecognised KPTN dosage-sensitivity, resulting in increased head circumference in heterozygous carriers of pathogenic KPTN variants. Molecular and structural analysis of Kptn-/- mice revealed pathological changes, including differences in brain size, shape, and cell numbers primarily due to abnormal postnatal brain development. Both the mouse and differentiated iPSC models of the disorder display transcriptional and biochemical evidence for altered mTOR pathway signalling, supporting the role of KPTN in regulating mTORC1. By treatment in our KPTN mouse model, we find that the increased mTOR signalling downstream of KPTN is rapamycin sensitive, highlighting possible therapeutic avenues with currently available mTOR inhibitors. These findings place KPTN-related disorder in the broader group of mTORC1 related disorders affecting brain structure, cognitive function, and network integrity.","author_count":44,"contributors":["Levitin, Maria O","Rawlins, Lettie E","Sanchez-Andrade, Gabriela","Arshad, Osama A","Collins, Stephan C","Sawiak, Stephen J","Iffland, Phillip H","Andersson, Malin H L","Bupp, Caleb","Cambridge, Emma L","Coomber, Eve L","Ellis, Ian","Herkert, Johanna C","Ironfield, Holly","Jory, Logan","Kretz, Perrine F","Kant, Sarina G","Neaverson, Alexandra","Nibbeling, Esther","Rowley, Christine","Relton, Emily","Sanderson, Mark","Scott, Ethan M","Stewart, Helen","Shuen, Andrew Y","Schreiber, John","Tuck, Liz","Tonks, James","Terkelsen, Thorkild","van Ravenswaaij-Arts, Conny","Vasudevan, Pradeep","Wenger, Olivia","Wright, Michael","Day, Andrew","Hunter, Adam","Patel, Minal","Lelliott, Christopher J","Crino, Peter B","Yalcin, Binnaz","Crosby, Andrew","Baple, Emma L","Logan, Darren W","Hurles, Matthew E","Gerety, Sebastian S"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-699984"],"filters":{"added":"2023-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Baple, Emma L","Gerety, Sebastian S"],"country":["Canada","France","Netherlands","United Kingdom","United States"],"glorg":["Alder Hey Children's Hospital","All Global Affiliations","Children's National Medical Center","Erasmus University Medical Centre","Johns Hopkins University","Leiden University","London Health Sciences Center, London, Ontario, Canada.","Oxford University Hospitals NHS Foundation Trust","University of Cambridge","University of Exeter","University of Groningen","University of Leicester","Université Bourgogne Europe","Université de Strasbourg","Wellcome Sanger Institute"],"keyword":["Animal model","Animals","Brain metabolism","Cognition","Humans","Macrocephaly","Mice","Microfilament Proteins genetics","Neurodevelopmental disorders","Recessive","Signal Transduction genetics","TOR Serine Threonine Kinases metabolism","iPSC","mTOR"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"OT":["Unspecified organisation"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"models of i kptn i related disorder implicate mtor signalling in cognitive and overgrowth phenotypes","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pu